WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.830
- [Marques] What's your name?

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:04.020
- [Siri] I'm Siri, pleased to meet you.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:06.330
- [Google] My nickname
is the Google Assistant.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:07.920
- [Bixby] The name is Bixby.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.095
- [Alexa] I'm Alexa.

00:00:09.095 --> 00:00:11.520
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.020
- I've talked and made jokes in the past

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.840
mostly along the lines of
Google Assistant is clearly

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.290
the best virtual assistant
and then Bixby, Siri

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:22.380
and Alexa are various
levels of far behind.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:26.460
But last time I did this
type of test was 2017

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.310
so I figured we should
revisit this a little bit,

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.200
retest things and figure out

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.630
if Google Assistant still actually is

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:37.080
the best virtual assistant
on a smartphone right now.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.200
Also shout out to Clippy, the
original virtual assistant

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.300
whose ugly Christmas sweater for Microsoft

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:45.930
is actually what inspired
this one, the MKBHD 8-bit Drip

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:47.400
which you can get wall supplies last.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:48.330
They're very limited

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:50.970
but they're at shop.mkbd.com, link below.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:53.550
But I decided to start
with the simple questions.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:56.250
You know The three things
that you ask from an assistant

00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:00.570
the most often I think are
the weather, timers and facts.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:02.490
So they all do fine with
the weather question,

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:04.590
probably the number one
query for me anyway.

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:05.430
And then for the most part,

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.650
they were also the same pretty quick

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.600
with just asking standard facts,

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.580
although for some reason they did disagree

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.500
on the height of one person.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:17.220
But then for timers, for
timers, for one weird reason

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:21.180
Siri cannot do multiple
timers at the same time

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.020
on the iPhone.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.260
If you have a timer running
and ask for a second timer,

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:28.050
it asks you every time
if you want to replace

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:28.950
the first timer.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:29.820
Pretty basic stuff

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:30.973
that I'm not sure why
Apple hasn't fixed yet.

00:01:30.973 --> 00:01:33.840
Especially because you
can set multiple timers

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.260
at the same time with
Siri on a HomePod mini.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:38.520
But anyway, I don't wanna make you watch

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.980
a whole bunch of back to back
to back questions and answers

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:43.800
because I admit it was kinda
boring when I was doing

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.950
all of that testing but I'm
going to share some examples

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.320
of what I found with you and
then some of the highlights

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.303
of the testing and the way
it answers these questions

00:01:51.303 --> 00:01:52.380
when you interact with them.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:53.790
So here we go.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:56.910
So with all the latest software
updates on every assistant,

00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:59.730
all being asked the exact same questions,

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:03.180
asked to do the exact same
tasks with the same phrasing

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.160
and on the same WiFi connections,

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.890
most often the fastest one to answer

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:10.170
was Google Assistant or Siri.

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:14.220
But, but Siri and Bixby, in my experience,

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:16.800
did have the most
instances of like hanging

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.470
and pausing and not really
answering a question.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.720
Kinda like acting like they
had a poor internet connection

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:22.560
even though they didn't.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:23.970
Not sure why they did that.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:25.712
A restart of the phone
helped with the Bixby one.

00:02:25.712 --> 00:02:27.450
Siri just kinda does it randomly,

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.250
not sure why but that's a real fact.

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:31.410
But then with straight up fact questions,

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:32.820
there is no runaway winner

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.140
which I think is a good thing actually.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.600
So if you ask something
that's easily Googleable

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.590
or on Wikipedia, something
like how tall is the Taj Mahal?

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:41.880
They can all get it.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.070
Or if you ask, what do whales eat?

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:45.870
They all had some type of answer for me

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:47.280
that was basically the same.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.400
Siri I found relies the
most heavily on Wikipedia

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.680
as a source for almost all of its answers.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.660
And then when it does have
to go outside of that,

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.820
it just kinda throws up a web search link

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.650
often with Wikipedia at the top,

00:02:58.650 --> 00:02:59.910
says, "Look what I Googled for you."

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:02.460
Now Google Assistant is
the most likely to give you

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:05.010
a nice visual and
slightly more information

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:05.880
than you asked for.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:08.670
Bixby is powered by Google

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:11.040
so I would say it's
the most likely to read

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.230
what Google assistant reads word for word.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:15.660
And then Alexa is the
most likely to throw in

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:19.020
some extra information about
your latest Amazon order

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:19.853
or something random.

00:03:19.853 --> 00:03:22.110
So facts, they can all do, great.

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:25.890
Then there is what I'd call
the device control category

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:28.860
because these are assistants
that are on your smartphones,

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.140
they're smart speakers as
well but on your phone,

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:32.490
you'd like to be able to
help you out with things

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:34.140
while you can't use your hands.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:37.500
And for this, there was a
runaway winner, actually two

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.560
and that would be Google
Assistant and Bixby.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.500
So with the most basic, basic stuff,

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:45.420
they're pretty much all fine.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.850
They can all change their
screen brightness, for example,

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:48.930
except Alexa.

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:50.910
But they can also all open the camera

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:53.220
or pretty much any app on your
phone with a voice command.

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:54.053
That's easy.

00:03:54.053 --> 00:03:55.710
Setting an alarm is light work.

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:58.410
If you ask it actually
to open the app store,

00:03:58.410 --> 00:03:59.970
they all open their respective app stores

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:03.540
and actually Bixby opens
the Samsung Galaxy Store

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:05.250
which is kinda cheeky.

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.350
But when it starts to get more complex,

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:09.630
Google and Bixby start to
walk away from the others.

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:11.910
So if I ask it to take a selfie,

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:14.220
Siri and Alexa will just open the camera

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.100
but Bixby and Google
actually open the camera,

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:18.930
switch to the selfie camera

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:22.080
and start a self-timer and
actually take a selfie.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:25.020
If I wanna say play
"Squid game" on Netflix,

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:28.920
both Google and Bixby will
actually open the Netflix app,

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:31.320
find the first episode of
"Squid Game" I haven't seen yet

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:33.900
or wherever I left off
and start playing it.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:37.200
Siri just opens the app and Alexa is lost.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:40.560
If I say start a voice recording
or a voice memo for Siri,

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.980
again, Google and Bixby
open the voice recording app

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:46.380
and actually start that recording.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.510
Siri again just opens the app.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:50.610
And again, Alexa is lost.

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:53.100
These voice commands when they
work are legitimately faster

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:54.750
than I would've been able to do it by hand

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:55.583
for the most part.

00:04:55.583 --> 00:04:57.450
And there's a bunch of
other device specific stuff

00:04:57.450 --> 00:05:00.270
that Samsung has programmed
into Bixby where it can reach

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:03.690
into apps and perform
specific commands within them.

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:05.880
So if there are apps who use a lot,

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:07.590
this can potentially
save you a lot of time.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.450
Bixby was also great at
this last time I did it

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:13.560
and also Alexa is unsurprisingly
the worst at device control

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:16.350
because they aren't
making their own phone.

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:17.580
These aren't Amazon phones.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.860
So the vertical
integration's great with Siri

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:22.890
and Google Assistant and
Bixby, not with Alexa,

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:24.060
not a shocker there.

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:26.640
What might be a little
bit of a surprise though

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:28.410
is conversationalism.

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:30.813
Who is the coach of the Phoenix Sons?

00:05:32.416 --> 00:05:34.265
Huh, how tall is he?

00:05:34.265 --> 00:05:37.348
(soft upbeat music)

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:40.766
Did he play in the NBA?

00:05:40.766 --> 00:05:43.849
(soft upbeat music)

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:46.430
What team did he play for?

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:48.360
(soft upbeat music)

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:49.710
So you can see, Siri tapped out

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:51.870
after about four questions
in a row about the same thing

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:54.270
but Google Assistant
is incredible at this.

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:57.270
It's really, really good
at remembering the context

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:59.070
and the subject of a natural conversation

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:02.010
and carrying that on through
a bunch of questions in a row.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:03.763
Where is the Taj Mahal?

00:06:03.763 --> 00:06:07.050
(soft upbeat music)

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:09.060
Okay, how tall is it?

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:12.143
(soft upbeat music)

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:15.891
How many meters is that?

00:06:15.891 --> 00:06:18.974
(soft upbeat music)

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:21.396
What is it made of?

00:06:21.396 --> 00:06:24.479
(soft upbeat music)

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:28.230
See, again, Google remembers
what we're talking about.

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:30.147
When is the Weeknd's birthday?

00:06:30.147 --> 00:06:33.230
(soft upbeat music)

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:36.970
What's their latest album?

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:40.053
(soft upbeat music)

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:43.825
What's his real name?

00:06:43.825 --> 00:06:46.680
(soft upbeat music)

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.350
So basically, if you're asking
a bunch of questions in a row

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:51.810
or if you have one subject that you want

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:54.360
to basically research
through the voice assistant

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.100
or get a bunch of answers on,

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:58.710
your best bet is definitely
Google Assistant.

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:00.300
And then one more interesting
thing from the crew

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.460
is that I ask just as
far as getting pictures.

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:03.990
When you ask for pictures of something

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:06.630
like dogs for example,
they can all search the web

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:07.770
except Bixby didn't.

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:10.740
And Alexa, which is looking
for an Amazon Photos app

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:11.970
for some reason.

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:15.870
But when you say, show
me my pictures of dogs,

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:19.770
Google actually opens and
searches in my own Photos album

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:21.330
for my own pictures of dogs.

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:22.920
So I did a bunch of
other testing like this

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:24.270
with a bunch of other different questions,

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:25.560
different styles of questions,

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:27.690
follow up questions and things like that

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:30.510
and I've come to a
pretty solid conclusion.

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:33.067
So if you're wondering
which one is the best;

00:07:33.067 --> 00:07:34.297
"Marques, you did all these tests,

00:07:34.297 --> 00:07:36.660
"which one came out as number one?"

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:39.060
Only one thing became exceptionally clear

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:42.750
through all this testing
which is that Alexa

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:46.710
is clearly the worst, is
clearly far behind the others

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:48.738
as a smart assistant on a phone.

00:07:48.738 --> 00:07:52.560
I mean, unless maybe you're already deep

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:55.290
in the Amazon Alexa ecosystem

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:57.420
with everything working
perfectly in your smart home.

00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:00.810
Matter of fact, this really
is an ecosystem question

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:03.690
almost as much as it is a skills question

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:04.523
for the assistants.

00:08:04.523 --> 00:08:07.080
Because if you are super all in

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:09.120
on one of these smart home ecosystems,

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:11.220
that actually could answer
the question for you.

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:12.990
Of course, if you're all in with HomeKit,

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:15.930
then Siri and then iPhone
is your only option

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:18.570
to view your cameras and
see your doorbell ringing

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:20.190
and turn on your lights
and all that stuff.

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:22.860
But if you are doing the
same with Google Home

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:24.630
and Google Assistant, then you're there.

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:28.680
Now the next Gen Matter
standard may change all of this.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:31.470
It may actually make
this section irrelevant,

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:33.360
maybe leave the thumbs up
on this video or comment

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:35.640
if you're interested in a
video about that specifically.

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:39.660
But for now, with these different
silos of home ecosystems,

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:40.493
that's pretty important.

00:08:40.493 --> 00:08:43.380
But I'll still say from
my most common experiences

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:46.230
and queries and the things
that are most useful;

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:49.560
across every single phone,
iPhones or Android phones,

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:53.430
the most useful assistant
is still Google Assistant.

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:55.920
And that's multiplied if
you also happen to have

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:59.370
a pixel phone because then
you also get call screening

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:02.070
and Hold for Me and all
those extra pixel features.

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:05.130
But in general, Google Assistant
is most likely to be fast.

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:08.010
It's most likely to give a
good well sourced answer.

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:10.050
It's most likely to be conversational

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:12.000
and remember context longer.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:13.740
And it has a bunch of third party plugs,

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:15.240
tons of app support.

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:17.970
And I also happen to
have built my smart home

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:20.100
in the Google Home world so it's great.

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:24.660
Now for the second best, I'm
very tempted to say Bixby.

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:26.850
Of course though, this is only relevant

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:29.130
if you have a Samsung phone.

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:32.160
And also, it is basically
saying all the same

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:35.880
first query responses
that Google assistant was

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:39.420
minus the ability to
talk to a smart speaker

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:43.530
since Samsung would probably
like us to forget about that.

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:47.280
But I am gonna have to put
Siri in second place here.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:48.660
It's fine, it's okay

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:50.700
it's all right, it's fine, it's not great

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:53.580
but it is the most well
plugged into Apple's ecosystem.

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:55.110
So of course, if you're
doing HomeKit stuff,

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:56.820
it's gonna work well there.

00:09:56.820 --> 00:10:00.360
It does support a smart speaker
which can do multiple timers

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:02.730
if you're not doing stuff
all the time on your phone.

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:04.440
And it does support routines

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:07.230
which is like stacking a
bunch of different commands

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:09.900
into one action if you're
willing to dig through

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:12.990
the very convoluted Siri
shortcuts interface.

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:17.990
So that puts Bixby in third
place and Alexa is just bad,

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:19.890
it's just bad on a phone.

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:22.020
Like there was a phone a couple years ago,

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:24.870
an HTC phone where it was built in

00:10:24.870 --> 00:10:27.780
so there was Google Assistant
and Alexa side by side

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:30.270
on the same phone and it
worked kinda well back then

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:32.730
but that was years ago and
Alexa is just dead last

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:34.560
on a phone right now and Amazon knows it.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:36.780
Amazon is falling behind with Alexa

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:39.240
and if you're just
starting to use assistants

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:41.490
or put together a smart
home or this type of stuff,

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:44.520
I would say avoid things that
are Alexa exclusive right now.

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:46.140
But until then, let me know if you agree

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:48.510
that Google Assistant still
seems pretty far out in front

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:50.520
or maybe there are some
specific features that you find

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:53.010
the most useful in a virtual assistant.

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:53.940
Let me know in the comments.

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:55.980
Either way, channel sponsor Dbrand,

00:10:55.980 --> 00:10:58.200
also would like you to know
that they are relaunching

00:10:58.200 --> 00:10:59.310
the Damascus skin.

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:01.110
They're bringing it back
due to popular demand.

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:04.920
It's got all this like crazy
reflective holographic chaos.

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:07.080
You can kinda skin whatever
device you want too

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:09.810
so you can make it work and you
get a free Triple Black skin

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:11.850
with your order, which is
sick, I'll link it below.

